DOGE-89 — Governmental Status, Sovereign-Immunity Defenses, Special Government Employees, Private Technology Interests, and the Alleged Conversion of Silicon Valley Liability into Protected Federal Conduct

Investigative Proposition

UNIKORNAIO alleges that DOGE represented more than an efficiency initiative. It may have functioned as a legal and institutional “Hail Mary” intended to place politically connected Silicon Valley personnel, technology companies, data operations, and restructuring activities behind the protective barriers of the federal government at a time when those interests faced potentially enormous economic, regulatory, civil, and criminal exposure.

The theory requiring investigation is that private interests understood the accumulating consequences of alleged misconduct involving:

unregulated digital assets and capital flight;
market manipulation and undisclosed insider allocations;
artificial-intelligence financing and inflated valuations;
misuse of consumer and government data;
anticompetitive platform control;
election-related influence operations;
government-contracting conflicts;
regulatory violations;
sanctions and money-laundering risks;
labor displacement;
cybersecurity failures; and
the transfer of public functions into privately controlled technology systems.

According to the allegation, DOGE offered an emergency conversion mechanism:

Transform private technology actors into government personnel.
Transform private objectives into presidential directives.
Transform private data access into official government work.
Transform commercial restructuring into public administration.
Transform potential defendants into federal officers, employees, advisers, or contractors.
Then invoke sovereign immunity, official authority, executive privilege, state-secrets concerns, jurisdictional defenses, and federal representation when accountability follows.
Government affiliation does not automatically immunize Silicon Valley companies or their personnel. But it can materially change who may be sued, where a case may be heard, what claims may proceed, what administrative prerequisites apply, what records may be obtained, and whether the United States replaces an individual defendant.
That legal transformation is the center of DOGE-89.
DOGE Was Created Through an Existing Governmental Shell
On January 20, 2025, an executive order publicly renamed the existing United States Digital Service as the United States DOGE Service and placed it within the Executive Office of the President.
The order also created the U.S. DOGE Service Temporary Organization under 5 U.S.C. § 3161 and required federal agencies to establish DOGE teams containing at least four personnel. Those teams could include special government employees and were generally expected to include:

a team leader;

an engineer;

a human-resources specialist; and

an attorney.

The order directed agencies to provide USDS with “full and prompt access” to unclassified agency records, software systems, and information-technology systems, to the maximum extent consistent with law.

It further purported to displace prior executive orders and regulations subject to presidential amendment when those authorities impeded DOGE access. At the same time, it stated that implementation must remain consistent with applicable law. Executive order establishing DOGE
The structure was significant. Instead of creating an ordinary private presidential commission, DOGE personnel were inserted into an existing federal entity, placed inside agencies, given government accounts or credentials, and assigned functions affecting public records, contracts, personnel, benefits, payments, and regulatory operations.
That structure potentially supplied private-sector participants with arguments that their actions were governmental acts undertaken within assigned federal duties.
Sovereign Immunity Is Not Automatically Transferable

Sovereign immunity generally protects the United States and its agencies from suit unless Congress has waived that immunity.

Private companies do not acquire sovereign immunity merely because their executives advise the President, their employees join DOGE, or their products are used by the government. Nor does calling a private objective a government initiative erase liability for conduct occurring before government service.

The following remain legally distinct:

the United States;

a federal agency;

a federal officer sued in an official capacity;

a federal employee sued individually;

a special government employee;

a private government contractor;

the employee’s private-sector employer; and

affiliated investors, companies, funds, or business partners.

Whether immunity applies depends on the defendant, the claim, statutory waivers, the employment relationship, the scope of assigned duties, and the specific conduct alleged.

DOGE therefore could not lawfully provide blanket immunity to Silicon Valley.

The more precise allegation is that governmental placement could be used to construct multiple defensive layers that make private accountability slower, more expensive, and procedurally difficult.

The Westfall Act and Substitution of the United States

The Federal Employees Liability Reform and Tort Compensation Act—commonly called the Westfall Act—provides that the Federal Tort Claims Act remedy against the United States is generally exclusive for certain common-law torts committed by federal employees acting within the scope of federal employment.

When the Attorney General certifies that an employee acted within the scope of employment:

a state-court case may be removed to federal court;

the United States may be substituted for the individual; and

the action becomes subject to Federal Tort Claims Act limitations and exceptions.

The Westfall Act does not ordinarily eliminate authorized constitutional claims or federal statutory claims against individuals. Scope-of-employment certification may also be challenged in court. 28 U.S.C. § 2679

Nevertheless, substitution can be decisive. After substitution, a plaintiff may face:

administrative-exhaustion requirements;
shortened filing periods;
restrictions on available damages;

immunity for particular intentional torts;

exceptions covering discretionary governmental functions;

limits on jury trials;
dismissal where Congress has not waived immunity; and
disputes over whether the claim is cognizable against the United States at all.
A Silicon Valley participant treated as a federal employee or special government employee might therefore argue that allegedly wrongful conduct was performed within an official DOGE assignment and that the United States—not the individual—must answer for it.
That possibility makes personnel status, appointment documentation, assigned duties, and scope-of-employment decisions critical evidence.
Special Government Employee Status
Special government employee status allows the government to obtain temporary services from individuals who may retain substantial connections to private industry.
That arrangement is not inherently improper. It becomes dangerous when an individual:

retains financial interests in affected companies;

participates in decisions benefiting those companies;

receives access to competitor or regulatory information;

influences investigations involving private associates;

shapes procurement specifications favoring affiliated technology;

accesses government data useful to a private business;
performs governmental and private functions simultaneously; or
later claims federal protection for actions advancing private interests.
Special government employees remain subject to applicable ethics and conflict-of-interest laws. Their appointment cannot lawfully convert private self-dealing, bribery, theft, unauthorized disclosure, or actions beyond delegated authority into protected federal conduct.
Investigators must determine whether governmental status was assigned because the individual was genuinely needed for a public function—or because the status supplied access, credibility, legal representation, and potential immunity defenses without requiring complete separation from private interests.
Government-Contractor Immunity Is Limited
Private contractors may sometimes assert derivative immunity or federal-preemption defenses when they perform work validly authorized by the federal government and comply with the government’s directions.
That protection is not unlimited.

The Supreme Court has explained that a contractor does not retain derivative sovereign immunity when it exceeds lawful authority or violates the government’s instructions. In Campbell-Ewald Co. v. Gomez, the Court rejected the proposition that federal contractors automatically share the government’s unqualified immunity. Supreme Court decision

Private actors therefore cannot manufacture immunity by:

obtaining a government contract after misconduct occurred;

placing their personnel inside an agency;

labeling unauthorized conduct “modernization”;

violating statutory privacy restrictions;

exceeding the limits of system access;

using public data for private purposes;
concealing conflicts of interest; or
acting outside the government’s lawful instructions.
The question is whether DOGE’s broadly worded mission and decentralized teams were used to create ambiguity about who authorized particular actions and whether those actions were governmental or private.
The “Goldilocks” Status Problem
DOGE’s legal position repeatedly generated disputes about what kind of entity it actually was.
When seeking agency access or directing governmental work, DOGE could be characterized as an official federal organization. When confronted with transparency demands, the government argued that USDS operated within the Executive Office of the President and was not necessarily an “agency” subject to the Freedom of Information Act.
A federal district court described the resulting position as a possible “Goldilocks” arrangement—an entity treated as an agency when governmental authority was advantageous but not treated as one when transparency obligations became burdensome.

That disputed status is directly relevant to the immunity theory.

An organization cannot legitimately claim:

government authority when demanding records;

presidential proximity when overriding agency resistance;

employee status when seeking federal legal protection;

advisory status when avoiding appointments scrutiny;

non-agency status when resisting FOIA; and

temporary status when preservation or accountability is demanded.
DOGE’s legal identity must be determined by its actual functions, authority, personnel, supervision, funding, and conduct—not whichever description produces the most favorable result in a particular lawsuit.
Prospective Immunity Versus Prior Misconduct
Government service cannot retroactively immunize private misconduct.
Any investigation must separate the relevant periods:
Before DOGE

Conduct occurring before federal appointment or assignment remains private conduct unless an independently applicable defense exists.

This includes potential liability arising from:

earlier digital-asset transactions;

securities or commodities misconduct;

consumer deception;

antitrust violations;

unauthorized surveillance;

data misuse;
election activity;
interference with investigations;
prior contracting misconduct; and
misappropriation of another person’s work or intellectual contribution.
During DOGE
Conduct performed during government service requires examination of whether it was:
within validly assigned duties;
properly authorized;
consistent with federal law;

affected by private financial conflicts;

performed for a public purpose;

recorded through official systems; and
separated from the participant’s private enterprise.
After DOGE
Post-government conduct must be reviewed for:
use of nonpublic federal information;
commercial exploitation of government-developed systems;
follow-on contracts;

investment or trading decisions based on official access;

solicitation of former agency contacts;

retention of federal data;
revolving-door violations; and
attempts to characterize subsequent private conduct as a continuation of protected government work.
No appointment, executive order, advisory title, or government credential should erase the boundary between these three periods.
Knowledge and Intent
The title’s allegation of “knowingly and willingly” creating a governmental protection structure requires proof of intent.
That proof could include communications showing that participants discussed:
potential personal or corporate liability;

obtaining federal representation;

scope-of-employment certification;

using special government employee status;

avoiding civil discovery;

placing work inside the Executive Office of the President;
avoiding FOIA;
invoking executive privilege;
converting private personnel into federal personnel;
transferring controversial projects into government contracts;
preempting state-law claims;
using national-security designations;
limiting inspector-general jurisdiction;
protecting earlier conduct through pardons, settlements, dismissals, or regulatory retreat; or
ensuring the government absorbed costs while private companies retained profits.
Absent such evidence, protective intent remains an investigative allegation rather than a verified conclusion.
But sophisticated technology executives, government attorneys, venture-capital counsel, and presidential advisers would reasonably understand that becoming federal personnel or contractors could alter litigation exposure. That foreseeable legal effect warrants examination of whether it was merely incidental or deliberately sought.
The Hail-Mary Protection Theory
UNIKORNAIO alleges that Silicon Valley’s underlying exposure had become too large to resolve through ordinary lobbying alone.
The industry allegedly faced interconnected liabilities involving capital flight, financial instability, token manipulation, artificial-intelligence debt, privacy violations, mass surveillance, election influence, antitrust concentration, labor displacement, public-data exploitation, and regulatory noncompliance.

DOGE potentially offered a final defensive strategy:

secure direct access to presidential authority;

enter the government through a renamed federal service;

obtain official or special-government-employee status;

insert personnel into enforcement and regulatory agencies;

obtain access to investigations, contracts, payment systems, and government data;

remove or weaken the officials capable of establishing liability;

redirect government policy toward deregulation and contracting;

characterize the conduct as official federal modernization;

rely on the Department of Justice to defend governmental actions;

invoke immunity, jurisdictional, privilege, and secrecy doctrines; and

return to private enterprise with increased market power and government-derived commercial advantages.

If supported by internal records, this would not be ordinary regulatory capture. It would be the attempted conversion of private economic exposure into government-protected conduct.

Government Representation as a Material Benefit

Federal affiliation can provide benefits beyond formal immunity.

An individual treated as a federal officer or employee may receive:

representation by Department of Justice attorneys;

removal of litigation from state to federal court;

government-funded defense;

access to classified or privileged defenses;

official-capacity standing arguments;

limitations on depositions of senior officials;
administrative-exhaustion defenses;
arguments based on separation of powers;
access to federal records and witnesses;
government indemnification in some circumstances; and
control over which documents are deemed official, privileged, or sensitive.
These protections have substantial economic value.
If governmental status was deliberately used to shift private defense costs and liability risks onto taxpayers, that benefit must be treated as a measurable transfer of public value to private interests.
No Immunity for Ultra Vires or Criminal Conduct
Government status is not a lawful license to commit crimes.
Sovereign immunity does not authorize an individual to:

accept bribes;

commit fraud;

obstruct justice;

destroy federal records;

retaliate against witnesses;

steal government information;
disclose protected data without authorization;
manipulate public contracts for personal benefit;
make false statements;
violate criminal conflict-of-interest statutes; or
participate in a racketeering enterprise.
Similarly, official-capacity defenses do not automatically protect actions taken beyond statutory or constitutional authority.
The fundamental question is not whether a participant possessed a federal title. It is whether the challenged conduct was lawfully authorized, within the person’s duties, and undertaken for the United States rather than for an affiliated private enterprise.
Potential Protection of Corporate Affiliates
Even where an individual qualifies for a federal defense, that protection does not ordinarily extend automatically to the person’s private company, investors, business partners, or affiliated funds.
Investigators must therefore resist any attempt to collapse separate legal identities.

For each alleged act, the record must identify:

who performed it;

who directed it;

who paid the actor;

which equipment and accounts were used;

which entity received the resulting information;

whether the actor held federal status at the time;
whether the conduct fell within assigned duties;
which private parties benefited; and
whether the government later ratified conduct that was unauthorized when performed.
Corporate affiliates should remain responsible for their own prior and independent conduct even if an executive later entered government service.
RICO Relevance
Formation of a government organization is not itself a racketeering predicate. Sovereign-immunity arguments and government legal representation are also not inherently unlawful.
They become relevant to a RICO investigation if evidence shows that the governmental structure was used to:
conceal or continue predicate offenses;

obstruct investigations;

retaliate against witnesses;

acquire confidential enforcement information;

convert government data to private use;

fraudulently obtain contracts;
protect proceeds of unlawful activity;
transfer defense costs to the public;
eliminate regulatory threats; or
create an appearance of lawful authority for actions serving a private enterprise.
The alleged governmental shield would then constitute evidence of the enterprise’s structure, continuity, concealment, and protection mechanisms.
Required Evidence
UNIKORNAIO calls for preservation and examination of:
appointment documents for every DOGE participant;
special-government-employee determinations;

ethics agreements and financial disclosures;

recusals and conflict-of-interest analyses;

job descriptions and delegations of authority;

scope-of-employment certifications;
requests for Justice Department representation;
indemnification discussions;
communications concerning personal or corporate liability;
communications discussing sovereign, qualified, or derivative immunity;
DOGE organizational charts and reporting structures;
agreements with private technology companies;
government-contracting and procurement records;
records identifying equipment, email accounts, and messaging systems used;
pre-government and post-government communications with private employers;
information transferred from federal systems to private entities;
litigation-hold and preservation notices;
privilege logs;
FOIA classification analyses;
inspector-general jurisdictional determinations;
regulatory investigations affecting DOGE-connected interests;
enforcement matters terminated after DOGE access;
pardons, settlements, dismissals, and policy reversals benefiting connected parties; and
post-government employment, investments, contracts, and compensation.
UNIKORNAIO’s Position
UNIKORNAIO does not assert that accepting a government position creates guilt or that every government contractor is part of an unlawful enterprise.
UNIKORNAIO alleges that DOGE’s unusual mixture of federal authority, private-sector personnel, disputed organizational status, extensive data access, weak transparency, temporary appointments, regulatory retreat, and enormous private conflicts created an unprecedented opportunity to convert private liability into government-defended conduct.
The legal issue must be stated precisely:
DOGE did not automatically give Silicon Valley sovereign immunity.
DOGE may have created positions, classifications, contracts, and procedural conditions through which connected private actors could seek federal protection, shift liability, restrict discovery, and characterize privately beneficial conduct as official government action.

That possibility is not speculative as a matter of legal mechanics. Whether it was an intentional purpose of DOGE depends on the internal evidence.

Requested Corrective Measures

UNIKORNAIO calls for:

independent review of every DOGE appointment and employment classification;

public disclosure of DOGE participants’ assigned duties and delegations;
identification of every request for federal representation or scope certification;

judicial review of disputed Westfall Act certifications;

denial of federal representation for conduct outside lawful government duties;

preservation of claims against private companies and affiliated persons;

prohibition against retroactive ratification of unauthorized private conduct;

recovery of public defense costs where government status was obtained through fraud or concealment;

audit of government information transferred to DOGE-connected companies;

enforcement of criminal conflict-of-interest laws;

review of contracts benefiting participants’ employers, investors, or affiliates;

disclosure of all immunity, indemnification, privilege, and preemption analyses;

preservation of state, Tribal, private, foreign, civil, criminal, and administrative enforcement authority;

rejection of any settlement or legislation granting retroactive amnesty;

disgorgement of private benefits derived from unauthorized government access; and

individual liability for ultra vires, fraudulent, obstructive, retaliatory, or criminal acts.

Preliminary Finding

The public record establishes that DOGE was placed inside an existing federal service, organized through the Executive Office of the President, staffed partly through agency-based teams that could include special government employees, and granted a government-wide mandate involving extensive access to federal records and systems.

The legal record also establishes that government employment and contracting can provide powerful procedural defenses. Those protections are conditional and do not automatically extend to private companies, conduct outside assigned duties, constitutional violations, authorized federal statutory claims, or unlawful acts exceeding government instructions.

The unresolved investigative question is whether these protections were incidental consequences of public service—or whether the governmental structure was knowingly designed as a last-resort shield for Silicon Valley actors facing the consequences of gross and egregious economic misconduct.

If the evidence establishes the latter, DOGE should be understood not merely as government capture, but as an attempted liability-conversion operation:

Private misconduct entered the government.
Government authority protected its continuation.
Public institutions absorbed the risk.
Private companies retained the benefit.
And sovereign power was invoked to prevent the public from obtaining a remedy.

